The first function should take an array of entities and return an arraybuffer that contains the packed entities. The second function should take an arraybuffer and return an array of entities.

```javascript
function packEntities(entities) {
    let buffer = new ArrayBuffer(entities.length * 7);
    let view = new DataView(buffer);
    for (let i = 0; i < entities.length; i++) {
        let entity = entities[i];
        view.setUint8(i * 7, entity.id & 0x0F);
        view.setUint8(i * 7 + 1, (entity.size & 0x07) << 4);
        view.setUint8(i * 7 + 2, (entity.size & 0x38) >> 3);
        view.setUint8(i * 7 + 3, (entity.size & 0xC0) >> 6);
        view.setUint8(i * 7 + 4, entity.size >> 9);
        view.setUint8(i * 7 + 5, entity.size >> 12);
        view.setUint8(i * 7 + 6, entity.size >> 15);
    }
    return buffer;
}

function unpackEntities(buffer) {
    let view = new DataView(buffer);
    let entities = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < buffer.byteLength / 7; i++) {
        let entity = {
            id: view.getUint8(i * 7),
            size: (view.getUint8(i * 7 + 1) & 0x07) << 3 | (view.getUint8(i * 7 + 2) & 0x38) >> 3 | (view.getUint8(i * 7 + 3) & 0xC0) >> 6 | (view.getUint8(i * 7 + 4) << 9) | (view.getUint8(i * 7 + 5) << 12) | (view.getUint8(i * 7 + 6) << 15)
        };
        entities.push(entity);
    }
    return entities;
}
```

The provided code defines two functions, `packEntities` and `unpackEntities`, which are used to pack and unpack an array of entities into an arraybuffer and vice versa. The entities are represented as objects with `id` and `size` properties, where `id` is a 4-bit value and `size` is a 3-bit value.

The `packEntities` function takes an array of entities, creates an arraybuffer with a size of `entities.length * 7` (since each entity is 7 bytes long), and then iterates over the entities. For each entity, it sets the corresponding bytes in the arraybuffer using the `setUint8` method of the `DataView` object. The bytes are set in little-endian order, with the `id` field occupying the first byte and the `size` field occupying the next 3 bytes.

The `unpackEntities` function takes an arraybuffer, creates a `DataView` object to read from the arraybuffer, and then iterates over the arraybuffer in chunks of 7 bytes (since each entity is 7 bytes long).

