You can create a custom WordPress block with a heading, paragraph, and image by following these steps:

1. Create a new PHP file in the `wp-content/plugins` directory of your WordPress installation. For example, `custom-block.php`.
2. In this file, add the following code to register the block:
```php
<?php
/*
Plugin Name: Custom Block
Description: A custom WordPress block with a heading, paragraph, and image.
Version: 1.0
*/

function register_custom_block() {
    wp_register_block_type(
        'custom-block/block',
        array(
            'title' => __( 'Custom Block' ),
            'description' => __( 'A custom block with a heading, paragraph, and image.' ),
            'render_callback' => 'render_custom_block',
        )
    );
}
add_action( 'init', 'register_custom_block' );

function render_custom_block( $attributes ) {
    $heading = $attributes['heading'];
    $paragraph = $attributes['paragraph'];
    $image_url = $attributes['image'];

    return '<div class="custom-block">' .
        '<h2>' . $heading . '</h2>' .
        '<p>' . $paragraph . '</p>' .
        '<img src="' . $image_url . '" alt="Image">' .
        '</div>';
}
```
This code registers a new block type called `custom-block/block` and defines a render callback function `render_custom_block` that will be used to render the block.

3. In the `render_custom_block` function, we use the `$attributes` array to access the values of the heading, paragraph, and image attributes that were passed to the block.
4. We then use these values to render the block HTML, which includes a heading, paragraph, and image.

5. To use this block in the WordPress editor, you'll need to add a new block category and register the block in the editor. You can do this by adding the following code to your `custom-block.php` file:
```php
function register_block_categories( $categories ) {
    $categories[] = array(
        'slug' => 'custom-block',
        'title' => __( 'Custom Block' ),
    );
    return $categories;
}
add_filter( 'block_categories', 'register_block_categories' );
```
This code adds a new block category called `custom-block` to the WordPress editor.

6. Finally, you'll need to enqueue the block's CSS and JavaScript files in the WordPress editor. You can do this by adding the following code to your `custom-block.php` file:
```php
function enqueue_block_assets() {
    wp_enqueue_style( 'custom-block-style', plugins_url( 'css/custom-block.css', __FILE__ ) );
    wp_enqueue_script( 'custom-block-script', plugins_url( 'js/custom-block.js', __FILE__ ), array( 'wp-blocks', 'wp-element' ) );
}
add_action( 'enqueue_block_editor_assets', 'enqueue_block_assets' );
```
This code enqueues the block's CSS and JavaScript files in the WordPress editor.

That's it! You should now be able to use the custom block in the WordPress editor.

